  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 120 AM EST Mon Nov 2 2026\n\nScattered, light rain showers/drizzle will persist through today as \nbroad 295-300K isentropic upglide continues. This isentropic ascent \nis very gentle though and we are still battling some dry air \nconcerns so there will be plenty of rain-free areas as well and have \nmaintained just broad-brushed 20 PoP's through this evening. Best \nchances appear to be east of I-69 late this afternoon into the \nevening as models indicate another weak shortwave rippling through \nthe area. This wave exits overnight and isentropic surfaces orient \nmore parallel to increasing southwest flow. Therefore, expect dry \nconditions to return late tonight through most of the day Tue. \nOvercast skies today yield highs only in the low/mid 60s. Tue high \ntemp forecast is tricky. Strong SW flow/WAA develops ahead of \napproaching trough but cloud cover is still a question. Some peaks \nof sun are likely, especially in our SE, and this could push highs \nto near record territory (76). Tempered NBM highs downward a bit as \nthey are likely biased by the last few weeks of very warm/sunny \nconditions. If full sun materializes then it's possible but expect \nat least some clouds given degree of moisture streaming into \nthe region.\n\nVery few changes regarding Tue night's frontal passage. Upper trough \nand low level cyclone will be rapidly lifting and occluding over the \nUpper Midwest late Tue. This combined with surface low track toward \nLake Superior will support a healthy midlevel dry slot moving into \nthe area Wed morning and bringing a quick end to rain. Maintained \ncategorical PoP's out of respect for the moisture advection but \nwestward displacement of better mid/upper level dynamics and \nsouthward displacement of any meaningful instability will likely \nleave our area with just some brief, light rain showers late Tue \nnight into Wed morning. Rainfall amounts around 0.25\" with no \nthunder anticipated. While steady/moderate rain ends Wed afternoon, \na prolonged period of general cyclonic flow is expected to develop \nWed night into next week as several upper lows pinwheel through the \nGreat Lakes. This supports periodic low chances for light rain, \nparticularly downwind of still warm Lake Michigan. The only \nexception looks to be a brief break in between systems on Sat. \nTemps return to more seasonable values to end the week but still\ntrending slightly above normal with highs around 60 and lows \naround 40.",
